---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/site';
const project = projects.find((item) => item.slug === 'evilfont')!;
---
<BaseLayout title="EvilFont">
  <div class="container page">
    <p><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← Projects</a></p>
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header"><span class="eyebrow">{project.category} · {project.tags.join(' · ')}</span><h1>{project.name}</h1><p>{project.description}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={project.github}>View on GitHub</a></div></header>
    <section class="detail-intro"><div class="detail-stat"><strong>Systemless</strong><span>Deployment model</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>3 platforms</strong><span>Magisk · KernelSU · APatch</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>CLI</strong><span>Interactive installer</span></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Overview</span><h2>System customization without touching /system.</h2><p>EvilFont is a systemless Android module for custom Persian/Arabic typography and Apple Emoji integration. It targets rooted devices running Magisk, KernelSU, or APatch.</p><p>The project is built around a non-destructive deployment model: system fonts are replaced dynamically through the module layer instead of modifying the actual system partition.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Approach</span><h2>Simple installation, flexible choices.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Systemless deployment</h3><p>Changes are applied through the root module layer, keeping the underlying system partition untouched.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Multiple root platforms</h3><p>Supports Magisk, KernelSU, and APatch so the module can fit different modern rooted Android setups.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Interactive CLI</h3><p>A volume-key-driven installer lets users choose fonts, emojis, or both without a large UI workflow.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Typography focus</h3><p>Custom Persian and Arabic typography is combined with Apple Emoji integration for a cohesive system-level customization.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Technical design</span><h2>Root tooling as a deployment layer.</h2><p>EvilFont is less about a conventional Android application and more about understanding how rooted Android systems expose replaceable resources. The module structure keeps the customization reversible and isolated from the underlying system image.</p><p>The installer is deliberately lightweight: it handles selection and deployment, while the root framework provides the mechanism that makes the systemless change possible.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Supported targets</span><div class="tags">{['Magisk','KernelSU','APatch','Persian','Arabic','Apple Emoji','iOS 18.4','macOS 26'].map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}</div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Design decision</span><h2>Customize the system, don't rewrite it.</h2><p>The core choice is to keep the deployment non-destructive. A system customization tool should provide the desired result without requiring users to modify the actual system partition or maintain a fragile collection of manual changes.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={project.github}>Explore the source</a><a class="button" href="/projects/">More projects</a></div></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
